    <p class="kicker">For the woman whose flush no longer fades by lunch</p>

    <h1 class="hero-headline">A flush is not an event. It is a <em>construction site.</em></h1>

    <p class="standfirst"><strong>This is not a skincare article. It is a mechanism piece.</strong> What a facial flush actually does underneath — why some flushes stop resetting after thirty-five, and why the standard trigger list misses the corridor they all lead to.</p>

    <div class="byline">
      <span class="byline__name">Rosalia · Human Wiring desk</span>
      <span>Mechanism, not medical advice</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="hero-figure">
    <div class="plate">
      {%- if s.fig1 != blank -%}
        {{ s.fig1 | image_url: width: 1600 | image_tag:
             widths: '640, 960, 1280, 1600',
             sizes: '(min-width: 1152px) 1100px, 92vw',
             loading: 'eager',
             alt: 'Illustration of a dermal capillary network, fine and sparse on the left, dense and tortuous on the right.' }}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      <span class="fig-tag">Fig. 1</span>
      <span>A vessel bed drawn as it changes with years of frequent flushing: fine and evenly branched at the left, denser and more tortuous at the right. <em>Illustration of the mechanism described below, not a photograph of a patient.</em></span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <main class="article-body">

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The first thing to know about a facial flush is that the model most of us were taught is the wrong one. Flushing gets described as a transient vasomotor event: the vessel dilates, blood rushes in, colour appears, colour fades. A weather system. Something that passes. Something whose only cost is the discomfort of the minutes it is happening.</p>

      <p>That description was built on what can be seen with a lens and a stopwatch. What it misses is what happens underneath, over months, when the same vessels are asked to dilate again and again. That story is not a weather story. It is a construction story. And it changes what avoiding a trigger is worth, why some flushes stop resetting after thirty-five, and what a serum can and cannot do.</p>
    </div>

    <aside class="identify" role="note">
      <p class="identify__label">If any of this describes the last twelve months of your face, this article is why:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A flush that appears in a warm meeting room before anything is eaten or said.</li>
        <li>A tingling under a moisturiser marketed as gentle.</li>
        <li>Cheeks that stay hot for hours after the trigger has passed.</li>
        <li>A flush that no longer waits for a reason.</li>
        <li>A drawer of prescription gels, sample tubes and cleansers that each helped for two weeks and then stopped.</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <aside class="objections" role="note">
      <p class="objections__lead">You have tried a lot. Here is why each one plateaued — before anyone tells you it is your fault.</p>
      <dl>
        <div>
          <dt>Prescription gels</dt>
          <dd>Suppress vasodilation for a window. Do not touch the enzyme that decides how long each dilation lasts.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Barrier creams</dt>
          <dd>Repair the barrier. Do not touch the translator that turns barrier damage into vessel dilation.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Trigger avoidance</dt>
          <dd>Closes one door of eight. The corridor stays open, and the traffic reroutes.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Laser or IPL</dt>
          <dd>Reduces the vessels you can already see. Does not slow the signal that builds new ones.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </aside>

    <section class="section">
      <span class="section__num">I</span>
      <h2 class="section__title">A vessel that dilates and returns. A vessel that dilates and stays.</h2>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="no-indent">Two faces meet the same warm room. In one, the vessel widens, the colour arrives, and twenty minutes later the vessel has gone back to the diameter it started at. In the other, it has not. The wall is thinner. The machinery that normally cinches a capillary back to its resting width is not pulling as hard as it did.</p>

      <p>The flush in the second face is not longer because the trigger was worse. It is longer because the return no longer works. Same vessel. Same trigger. Two different endings. That difference is the whole subject of this piece.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="figure">
      <div class="plate">
        {%- if s.fig2 != blank -%}
          {{ s.fig2 | image_url: width: 1400 | image_tag:
               widths: '560, 900, 1120, 1400',
