    <section class="section">
      <span class="section__num">II</span>
      <h2 class="section__title">Each flush sends a growth signal. The vessels answer it.</h2>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="no-indent">A flush that fails to reset is read by the surrounding tissue as a small hypoxic event. Cells near a persistently dilated vessel release vascular endothelial growth factor — the same molecule the body uses during wound healing. New capillaries get built. Existing ones widen and twist. Over years, the vessel bed under skin that flushes frequently becomes structurally different from the vessel bed under skin that does not.</p>

      <p>This is why the redness of a woman at forty-two who has flushed since her late twenties is not simply the redness of a woman at twenty-eight, repeated. The architecture has changed. More vessels. Wider. More tortuous. Nothing else about the skin needs to have changed at all.</p>
    </div>

