    <section class="section">
      <span class="section__num">III</span>
      <h2 class="section__title">The thermostat used to reset. Then it stopped.</h2>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="no-indent">Women describing this change use almost identical words: <em>it used to fade by lunch; now it doesn't fade at all.</em> The trigger has not changed. The wine, the meeting room, the walk from the car park — the inputs are constant. What changed is that the reset no longer arrives.</p>

      <p>A large part of the mechanism turns out to be a single enzyme. Yamasaki and colleagues, in a 2007 <em>Nature Medicine</em> paper<a href="#hw-fn1" class="fn">1</a> that has since been cited thousands of times, showed that facial skin contains a serine protease called KLK5. In unremarkable skin its activity is tightly regulated. In rosacea-prone skin that regulation fails. KLK5 stays active. It activates cathelicidin. And cathelicidin is itself vasoactive: it opens vessels, and it keeps them open.</p>

      <p>The shape of a normal flush is a spike that returns to zero. The shape of a flush after this switch has failed is a spike that partially returns, then rises again over the following hours, and does not get back down before the next trigger arrives.</p>
    </div>

